= Waves 4–6 cross-wave audit =

'''State: post-merge cross-wave audit.'''

== Scope ==

This audit covers the three consecutive merged corpus waves completed in the 2026-09-19 session:

* [[BITwiki:Wave 4 context audit|Wave 4 — Cognition and cognitive bias]]: 23 concepts / 69 reader surfaces.
* [[BITwiki:Wave 5 context audit|Wave 5 — Logic and probability]]: 18 concepts / 54 reader surfaces.
* [[BITwiki:Wave 6 context audit|Wave 6 — Formal computation and computer-science foundations]]: 17 concepts / 51 reader surfaces.

Combined scope:

<pre>
58 canonical concepts
174 reader surfaces
Core + Simple + Technical
</pre>

The audit examines the merged <code>main</code> state, cross-wave semantics, graph connectivity, evidence quality, request/workflow state, and release bookkeeping.

== Audit method ==

The audit deliberately does not treat prior CI success as proof of editorial quality.

It rechecks four layers:

# '''Structural / identity''' — one Core semantic identity, derivative identity exemption, controlled Domains, source/depth templates.
# '''Cross-wave semantics''' — later concepts must not contradict or silently redefine earlier concepts.
# '''Knowledge graph connectivity''' — resolved concepts should be linked from the earlier pages that introduced or depended on them.
# '''Evidence / provenance''' — source-sensitive and contested claims require identifiable sources rather than only generic source-family labels.

The repository release gates remain the final machine validation after editorial corrections.

== Finding 1 — isolated waves passed, but the combined graph was underlinked ==

The largest defect was not a false definition. It was '''failure to recur after canonicalization'''.

Earlier wave capture files explicitly identified dependencies on concepts that were created in later waves, but many Core pages were never revisited once those targets became canonical.

Examples found on merged <code>main</code>:

* [[Reasoning]] did not link the now-canonical [[Deductive reasoning]], [[Inductive reasoning]], or [[Abductive reasoning]].
* [[Logic]] did not link the now-canonical [[Formal language]].
* [[Consistency (logic)]] did not connect to the later, distinct consistency usages in [[Database]] and [[Distributed system]].
* [[Distributed cognition]] and [[Distributed system]] were intentionally distinguished in audit prose but not mutually connected in the reader graph.
* [[Human-computer interaction]] used cognitive concepts but lacked the full intended cross-wave relationship to [[Cognition]] and [[Distributed cognition]].
* theoretical-computation pages did not consistently back-link to [[Algorithm]], [[Computation]], [[Programming language]], [[Syntax]], or [[Semantics]] despite those dependencies being captured earlier.

This is an editorial-loop failure:

<pre>
CANONICALIZE
→ TRANSCLUDE / RELATE
→ RECUR
</pre>

The canonicalization step occurred, but the relation/backfill step was incomplete.

== Graph repair ==

Forty-one Core pages across Waves 4–6 were updated with explicit, contextual cross-links to already canonical neighboring concepts.

High-value repaired paths include:

<pre>
Reasoning
 ├→ Deductive reasoning
 ├→ Inductive reasoning
 └→ Abductive reasoning

Cognitive bias
 └→ Probability / Uncertainty / Reasoning

Selection bias
 └→ Probability / Correlation / Conditional probability / Causality

Logic
 └→ Formal language / Syntax / Semantics / Inference

Probability
 └→ Probability distribution / Conditional probability / Evidence / Uncertainty

Consistency (logic)
 └→ Database / Distributed system
    (explicit non-equivalence)

Formal language
 └→ Syntax / Semantics / Programming language / Logic

Turing machine
 └→ Algorithm / Computation / Formal language / Computability

