The repair adds relationships; it does not create new Knowledge objects or duplicate semantic identity.

== Finding 2 — source specificity was uneven ==

The conceptual content was generally source-oriented, but citation quality varied.

Broad orientation pages sometimes used acceptable source-family references such as “standard texts”. That is not automatically a defect.

However, source-sensitive claims should not depend only on phrases such as:

* “contemporary reviews”;
* “replication-sensitive literature”;
* “later research”;
* “standard texts”.

The most important cases were corrected.

== Evidence-sensitive source repair ==

=== Cognitive load ===
The page now names:

* Sweller (1988), “Cognitive Load During Problem Solving: Effects on Learning”.
* Sweller, van Merriënboer & Paas (2019), “Cognitive Architecture and Instructional Design: 20 Years Later”.

This supports the article's history-sensitive treatment of intrinsic/extraneous load and the later reconceptualization of germane processing.

=== Priming ===
The page now separates classic cognitive priming from disputed behaviorally distant social-priming claims using identifiable sources:

* Meyer & Schvaneveldt (1971), semantic priming.
* Tulving & Schacter (1990), priming and memory systems.
* Doyen et al. (2012), direct replication / experimenter-expectancy issue.
* Pashler, Coburn & Harris (2012), failed social-distance priming replications.

The correction preserves:

<pre>
robust cognitive priming
≠ blanket validation of broad behavioral/social priming claims
</pre>

=== Theory of mind ===
The bibliography now anchors:

* Premack & Woodruff (1978);
* Baron-Cohen, Leslie & Frith (1985);
* Wellman, Cross & Watson (2001) meta-analysis;
* Heyes (2014) critical review of infant false-belief evidence.

This matches the article's explicit warning that false-belief tasks and infant implicit paradigms do not exhaust theory of mind.

=== Thought suppression / ironic-process theory ===
The pages now identify:

* Wegner et al. (1987), classic white-bear experiments;
* Wenzlaff & Wegner (2000), historical review;
* Niczyporuk (2025 online / 2026 volume), contemporary review of effectiveness and adaptiveness.

The modern review supports the Wave-4 correction that suppression can be effective and that paradoxical rebound is not established as a universal law.

[[Ironic process theory]] remains <code>Supported</code>, not <code>Established</code>.

=== Belief revision ===
The page now identifies the current 2026 substantive revision of the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry and the AGM primary paper.

This verifies that the page correctly treats AGM as foundational but not exhaustive, including belief bases, iterated revision, and non-prioritized change.

=== Turing completeness ===
The page now anchors the distinction between computational equivalence and the Church–Turing thesis to current SEP entries:

* “Turing Machines”, substantive revision 21 May 2025.
* “The Church–Turing Thesis”, substantive revision 18 December 2023.

The wave continues to preserve:

<pre>
formal equivalence theorem
≠ Church–Turing thesis
</pre>

=== Esoteric programming language ===
The article now gives the full Singer & Draper SPLASH 2025 citation and DOI.

This supports retaining esolangs as a public Computer science concept rather than treating them as mere novelty.

== Finding 3 — per-wave audit state was stale after merge ==

The Wave 4, Wave 5, and Wave 6 audit pages still ended with pre-merge language saying merge remained conditional.

That statement was historically correct before each merge but stale on current <code>main</code>.

Each audit page is updated by this cross-wave audit to record its actual merge state and point here for the post-merge combined review.

== Cross-wave semantic audit ==

=== Wave 4 → Wave 5 ===

[[Reasoning]] remains the cognitive umbrella.

Wave 5's deductive, inductive and abductive pages are descendants, not replacements.

[[Cognitive bias]] retains benchmark dependence and does not turn probability theory into a definition of rationality.

[[Selection bias]] remains methodological/statistical rather than cognitive.

=== Wave 5 → Wave 6 ===

[[Logic]] and [[Formal language]] are connected without equating them:

<pre>
formal language → structured strings/syntax
logic           → consequence/proof/semantics over formal expressions
</pre>

[[Consistency (logic)]] remains distinct from database and distributed-system consistency.

[[Propositional logic]], [[First-order logic]], and [[Modal logic]] remain formal logical systems rather than programming-language or workflow mechanisms.

=== Wave 4 → Wave 6 ===

[[Distributed cognition]] and [[Distributed system]] remain different units of analysis:

<pre>
distributed cognition → cognitive process across people/artifacts/environment
distributed system    → coordinated computation across networked components
</pre>

[[Human-computer interaction]] correctly depends on cognitive concepts without reducing HCI to cognitive psychology.

[[Working memory]] remains distinct from machine memory, context windows, and storage systems.

== Title and identity audit ==

No regression was found in the canonical title decisions:

* [[Context (linguistics)]] remains separate from generic context.
* [[Discourse (linguistics)]] remains separate from Discourse software.
* [[Consistency (logic)]] remains qualified.
* [[First-order logic]] remains canonical instead of ambiguous “Predicate logic”.
* [[Automaton (computation)]] remains qualified.
* “Pink elephant bias” remains noncanonical.

No duplicate semantic identity is introduced by this audit.

== Request/workflow audit ==

Wave 6's ten pre-existing Computer science requests remain intended as <code>satisfied</code> records pointing to canonical pages rather than active duplicate requests.

The workflow validator will be rerun after this cross-wave audit branch is complete.

== Surface-depth audit ==

This cross-wave repair targets Core graph connectivity.

Simple and Technical pages remain reading-depth derivatives of the same Core identity.

They are not required to duplicate every Core “Related concepts” edge; depth-aware links should continue to be used organically where those relationships are part of the explanation.

No derivative receives a duplicate <code>Knowledge object</code> or category identity.

== Remaining editorial debt ==

The source system still mixes full citations with source-family shorthand on broad, noncontroversial orientation pages.

That is acceptable under the present corpus style, but a future bibliography-normalization pass could standardize editions, years, DOI/ISBN metadata, and external-link formatting across the entire encyclopedia.

This is a formatting/provenance-improvement opportunity, not a blocker for Waves 4–6 correctness.

== Release criteria for this audit ==

The cross-wave audit is complete only when:

# the graph repairs remain valid MediaWiki;
# all three prior wave counts remain 23/69, 18/54, and 17/51;
# overall corpus remains 125 canonical concepts / 375 reader surfaces;
# no new duplicate Knowledge object is introduced;
# controlled Domain validation passes;
# request-workflow audit passes;
# Main-space identity audit passes;
# runtime-schema/substrate/deployment audits pass;
# staging projection builds successfully;
# this audit is merged into <code>main</code>.

